; blog.loon — Blog index page
[fn blog-features []
  [ul-el
    {:style {:margin-left "1.5rem" :color [color :muted] :line-height "1.8"}}
    [li-el "Tail call optimization (loop/recur, trampoline)"]
    [li-el "Persistent data structures (imbl)"]
    [li-el "Full interpreter with 50+ builtins"]
    [li-el "Hindley-Milner type inference"]
    [li-el "Ownership checking (inferred)"]
    [li-el "Algebraic effects with handle/resume"]
    [li-el "Multi-shot delimited continuations"]
    [li-el "Effect handler towers: one program, prod/test/replay"]
    [li-el "Loon OS: syscalls are effects, the kernel is a handler"]
    [li-el
      "Record/replay, deterministic simulation, agent containment as handlers"]
    [li-el "Async as effects: cooperative scheduler, structured concurrency"]
    [li-el "Real TCP/HTTP sockets — serve HTTP from Loon"]
    [li-el "HTTP framework with capability-typed routes"]
    [li-el "Agent framework: approvals, backtracking, durable resume"]
    [li-el "Pattern matching with guards"]
    [li-el "Pipe operator with thread-last semantics"]
    [li-el "Module system with selective imports"]
    [li-el "REPL with time travel and forking"]
    [li-el "Basic WASM codegen"]]]

[fn blog-article [title date & body]
  [article-el
    {:style {:display "grid"
        :grid-template-columns "140px 1fr"
        :gap "0 3rem"
        :padding "3.5rem 0"}}
    [div
      {:style {:position "sticky"
          :top "2rem"
          :align-self "start"
          :color [color :muted]
          :font-family [font :mono]
          :font-size "0.8rem"
          :letter-spacing "0.05em"
          :padding-top "0.35rem"}}
      date]
    [div
      {:style {:min-width "0"}}
      [h2
        {:style {:font-family [font :serif]
            :color [color :primary]
            :font-size "2.2rem"
            :font-weight "600"
            :line-height "1.2"
            :margin-bottom "1.5rem"}}
        title]
      [div
        {:style {:font-family [font :serif]
            :font-size "1.05rem"
            :line-height "1.8"
            :color [color :text]}}
        body]]]]

[fn blog-h3 [text]
  [h3
    {:style {:font-family [font :serif]
        :color [color :primary]
        :font-size "1.4rem"
        :font-weight "600"
        :margin-top "2.5rem"
        :margin-bottom "0.75rem"}}
    text]]

[fn blog-code [src] [code-block {} src]]

[fn blog-page []
  [div
    {:class "page changelog"}
    [main-el
      {:style {:padding "3rem 0"}}
      [h1
        {:style {:font-family [font :serif]
            :color [color :primary]
            :font-size "2.5rem"
            :font-weight "400"
            :margin-bottom "3.5rem"}}
        "Changelog"]
      
      ; ── v0.9.0 — Placement is an effect ────────────────────────────────
      [blog-article
        "Placement Is an Effect"
        "Aug 18, 2026"

        [p
          "A syscall is an effect, so the kernel is the outermost handler. Follow the same sentence one step further and the GPU becomes a handler in the middle. Where a kernel runs is no longer a property of the program — it is a decision somebody makes around it, and the program does not move."]

        [blog-h3 "A kernel, and nothing about where it runs"]
        [p
          "A kernel is an ordinary function that promised to stay small: no closures, no allocation, no strings, no effects. Nothing in it mentions a device, a queue, or a transfer."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "[kernel saxpy [i a x y out]\n"
            "  [put out i [+ [* a [at x i]] [at y i]]]]\n"
            "\n"
            "[Place.run saxpy n #[3.0 x y out]]\n"
            "[Place.read out]"]]
        [p
          "Which arguments travel to the device and which have to come home is inferred, not annotated. Loon already worked out, for every parameter, whether the body reads it, writes through it, or consumes it — the distinction Rust makes you spell as &T, &mut T, and T. Here `at` is a read and `put` is a write-through, so `out` is the one that needs synchronizing back, and the emitted shader binds it read_write while the inputs bind read."]

        [blog-h3 "Residency is nine lines"]
        [p
          "Because Place.run and Place.read are operations rather than function calls, a handler sees every launch and every synchronization point. That is enough to write a residency policy as ordinary library code — the transfer-hoisting a GPU compiler implements as an optimization pass."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "[fn place/resident [thunk]\n"
            "  [handle [thunk]\n"
            "    [Place.run k n args]\n"
            "      [do [Place.pin args]\n"
            "          [resume [Place.run k n args]]]\n"
            "    [Place.read b]\n"
            "      [do [let v [Place.read b]]\n"
            "          [Place.unpin b]\n"
            "          [resume v]]]]]"]]
        [p
          "On an Apple M4 Max through Metal, a chain of 128 launches over one buffer takes 356.7 ms with no policy and 18.3 ms under that handler — 19.5x, from 128 uploads down to 2. Same program, same answer; only the line wrapped around it differs."]

        [blog-h3 "Testing a GPU program without a GPU"]
        [p
          "Launches are events, so they can go on tape. Record a run on the GPU and replay it on a build with no GPU support compiled in at all — the program observes exactly what it observed the first time. A handler that declines to forward gives you a dry run: every accounting number, no execution."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "$ loon run samples/place/saxpy.oo --place gpu --record trace.oo\n"
            "out   = #[0 5 10 15 20 25 30 35]\n"
            "\n"
            "$ loon replay trace.oo samples/place/saxpy.oo   ; no GPU in this build\n"
            "out   = #[0 5 10 15 20 25 30 35]"]]

        [blog-h3 "Landing alongside"]
        [ul-el
          {:style {:margin-left "1.5rem" :line-height "1.8"}}
          [li-el
            "Kernels compile to WGSL and every one in the repo is validated by naga in CI, on machines with no GPU."]
          [li-el
            "Placed programs run in a browser: web/public/place.html picks a placement in a tab and shows what crossed the boundary. Reaching a GPU from there still needs wgpu's asynchronous device setup, so --place gpu refuses and says why."]
          [li-el
            "Reductions, with no new language feature: each work item sums its own chunk into its own slot, so the write-at-your-own-index rule holds throughout (samples/place/reduce.oo)."]
          [li-el
            "One shared value layout across the VM, WASM, and native backends, with a conformance test that compares raw bits — it found three real divergences the first time it ran."]
          [li-el
            "--place cpu|device|gpu and --place-stats, reporting launches, transfers, and bytes."]
          [li-el
            "Buffers: dense, unboxed numeric arrays that can leave the process, which persistent collections cannot."]]]

      ; ── Record/replay + capability security ────────────────────────────
      [blog-article
        "Record the Bug, Replay It Forever"
        "Jul 1, 2026"
        
        [p
          "Some bugs only happen on some runs: a clock lands on an unlucky value, a file changes between reads, a request races a timeout. In Loon all of that nondeterminism flows through effects — so it can be put on tape. This release makes determinism the debugging superpower and spells out the security story that comes from the same design."]
        
        [blog-h3 "loon run --record / loon replay"]
        [p
          "Pass --record to any run and every nondeterministic effect result — clock reads, uuids, file reads, env lookups, network calls, plus log writes for observability — is appended to a trace file in Loon data format. Entries are flushed as they happen, so the trace survives the crash it was recording. Then loon replay feeds the results back in order: same program, same trace, identical execution — including reproducing the crash at the same step with the same error report. Re-run it under scrutiny as many times as you like; the bug holds still."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "$ loon run samples/replay-demo.oo --record crash.oo\n"
            "processing batch (roll 0)\n"
            "corrupt record encountered!\n"
            "error: assertion failed: 0 != -1\n"
            "note: crash trace saved to crash.oo\n"
            "\n"
            "$ loon replay crash.oo samples/replay-demo.oo\n"
            "processing batch (roll 0)   ; same roll\n"
            "corrupt record encountered! ; same crash, every time"]]
        [p
          "The trace is just Loon data — a vector of maps you can read, diff, and check in. If the program changes and performs a different operation than the trace recorded, replay stops with a divergence diagnostic naming the expected vs requested op and the step index, instead of feeding it a wrong value."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "#[{:effect \"IO\" :op \"millis\" :args #[] :result 1751338712345}\n"
            "  {:effect \"IO\" :op \"println\" :args #[\"processing batch (roll 0)\"] :result :unit}]"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "The capability story, written down"]
        [p
          "docs/capabilities.md documents Loon's security model as it actually exists: effects make all ambient authority explicit, dependencies are pure by default, and pkg.oo grants effects per dependency. loon audit --capabilities prints the grant surface; the full audit walks the lockfile and flags transitive deps whose effect needs were never granted — an exit code you can gate CI on. A function that performs an undeclared effect fails loon check with E0401. The walkthrough shows a malicious transitive dependency being caught at three checkpoints before it can phone home."]
        
        [blog-h3 "Landing alongside"]
        [ul-el
          {:style {:margin-left "1.5rem" :line-height "1.8"}}
          [li-el
            "Cross-backend conformance suite: one corpus of programs, every backend must agree — divergence is a failing test."]
          [li-el
            "Effect-row polish in the checker: more precise inferred rows across handlers and higher-order code."]]]

      ; ── v0.8.0 — Loon OS: syscalls are effects ─────────────────────────
      [blog-article
        "The Kernel Is Just a Handler"
        "Jul 1, 2026"
        
        [p
          "A syscall is an effect. Follow that one sentence far enough and the kernel stops being privileged code behind a trap — it becomes the outermost handler wrapped around your program. Loon OS is that idea, shipped: an operating-system layer written entirely in Loon, where sandboxing, chroot, strace, record/replay, deterministic simulation, and agent containment are all the same move — wrap the program in a different handler."]
        
        [blog-h3 "The whole kernel"]
        [p
          "The kernel does nothing special. It is the last handler standing: it translates the Sys effects a program performs into real syscalls. That is the entire thing."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "[fn kernel [thunk]\n"
            "  [handle [thunk]\n"
            "    [Fs.read-file p]    [resume [IO.read-file p]]\n"
            "    [Fs.write-file p c] [resume [IO.write-file p c]]\n"
            "    [Clock.now]         [resume [IO.now]]\n"
            "    [Rand.int n]        [resume [imod [IO.millis] n]]\n"
            "    [Env.get k]         [resume [Env.get k]]]]"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "chroot is a library"]
        [p
          "A program never names its kernel — it just performs Fs effects. So containment is interposition: slip a handler between the program and the kernel that rewrites paths before forwarding them. Nesting sandboxes is handler composition. strace is the same shape with a log line. A read-only mount is the same shape with a denial."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "[fn sandboxed [thunk root]\n"
            "  [handle [thunk]\n"
            "    [Fs.read-file p]    [resume [Fs.read-file [str root p]]]\n"
            "    [Fs.write-file p c] [resume [Fs.write-file [str root p] c]]]]"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "Record a run, replay it exactly"]
        [p
          "Every source of nondeterminism — the clock, randomness, the results of IO — is an effect. So recording a run means logging each effect result to a tape; replaying means answering those same effects from the tape, with no kernel underneath. A production crash ships home as a tape and replays bit-for-bit, touching nothing real."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "recording (real IO, real clock):\n"
            "  live:   read 'hello from the past' at t=1782950917104 (roll: 104)\n"
            "replaying from tape (no kernel, no real IO):\n"
            "  replay: read 'hello from the past' at t=1782950917104 (roll: 104)\n"
            "REPLAY IDENTICAL"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "An agent's permissions are its handler stack"]
        [p
          "An untrusted agent is just a thunk, and its permission grant is the tower you run it under. A reference monitor consults a policy on every request; a denied op never executes — the agent gets an EACCES value back instead of a trap, so its own control flow stays intact. Run it inside a sealed simulation and the entire agent behavior — every attempt, allowed and denied — is audited and reproducible from a seed."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "; an agent tries to steal credentials and phone home\n"
            "answer file: '42 (compute the answer)'   ; honest work: done\n"
            "exfil file:  '<never written>'           ; theft: blocked\n"
            "audit log:\n"
            "  {:op :read  :path /etc/credentials :got EACCES: /etc/credentials}\n"
            "  {:op :write :path /evil/exfil      :got :denied}\n"
            "rerun, same seed: AGENT RUN REPRODUCED EXACTLY"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "What building it cost"]
        [p
          "The OS is eight small files and no new runtime primitives — but writing it flushed out two real bugs in the compiler, both now fixed and pinned with regression tests. The first time a handler forwarded an effect to the handler beneath it, the VM re-entered its own clause and looped forever; the fix was proper deep-handler semantics. And the supervision retry pattern silently corrupted itself, because try compiled its recovery closure with no variable capture. The interposition every one of these features stands on works now — and the register VM is the reference for what effects mean."]]

      ; ── v0.7.0 — Effects, towers, and two frameworks ───────────────────
      [blog-article
        "Effect Handlers, End to End"
        "Jun 18, 2026"
        
        [p
          "Loon's algebraic effects grew up. The same program now runs unchanged under different handler towers — production, test, and replay differ only in the handlers wrapped around the code, never in the code itself. On that substrate we built two libraries that share it: an HTTP framework and an agent framework."]
        
        [blog-h3 "Multi-shot continuations"]
        [p
          "The default VM now supports multi-shot delimited continuations: a handler can resume a captured continuation zero, one, or many times. Resuming the same point more than once explores several futures from a single program — the basis for nondeterministic search, backtracking, and retries."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "[effect Choice [pick [] Int]]\n"
            "[fn prog [] [let a [Choice.pick]] [let b [Choice.pick]] [+ [* a 10] b]]\n"
            "\n"
            "; resume each pick over {1,2,3} and sum all 9 worlds -> 198\n"
            "[handle [prog] [return x] x\n"
            "  [Choice.pick] [+ [resume 1] [+ [resume 2] [resume 3]]]]"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "One program, three towers"]
        [p
          "A program performs effects and never names a handler, so its inferred effect row advertises exactly what it can do. Swapping the handler tower swaps the implementation — a deterministic offline test, a recording replay, or real production IO — with zero changes to the program. There are no exceptions anywhere; Fail is an effect."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "[fn make-session-id []        ; row: #{Reader Clock Random Log Fail}\n"
            "  [let prefix [Reader.ask]]\n"
            "  [str prefix \"-\" [Clock.now] \"-\" [Random.gen]]]\n"
            "\n"
            "[under-test make-session-id]  ; deterministic, offline\n"
            "[under-prod make-session-id]  ; real clock/uuid/env"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "Async is an effect"]
        [p
          "Concurrency is ordinary code performing effects, with the scheduler as a handler — no async function coloring, no separate runtime. A cooperative scheduler built on multi-shot continuations provides fork, yield, and cancel, with structured concurrency: the scheduler is the scope, and cancelling it cancels its children."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "[fn worker [t] [println [str t \" 1\"]] [Co.yield] [println [str t \" 2\"]]]\n"
            "[scheduler [fn [] [spawn [fn [] [worker \"A\"]]] [spawn [fn [] [worker \"B\"]]]]]\n"
            "; A 1 / B 1 / A 2 / B 2"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "Real HTTP, served from Loon"]
        [p
          "TCP sockets are wired into the VM, so a Loon program can serve real HTTP: listen on a port, accept a request, send a response. The server is the bottom handler; request handlers are effectful functions whose rows show their capabilities — a route cannot touch the database without Db appearing in its signature."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "loon run src/http/serve.oo\n"
            "curl localhost:8080/dashboard\n"
            "; -> prod-user sees live-row-1, live-row-2"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "An HTTP framework"]
        [p
          "Routes are effectful functions; the handler tower is the application. The same app runs under a deterministic test tower (no network, no mocks), a trace tower that records every effect, and a prod tower over a real socket. The response body is moved into the responder and consumed on send, so sending twice is a compile error."]
        
        [blog-h3 "An agent framework"]
        [p
          "The agent control loop is plain code performing Llm, Tool, Approval, and Memory effects; the tower decides everything else. The same loop runs as a deterministic offline eval, a full trace, a human-in-the-loop approval round-trip, and — via multi-shot — an explorer that resumes each approval both ways to visit every outcome. Durable execution falls out as a handler: replay a journal of effect results, then go live, so a crashed run resumes where it left off. The agent can even be served over HTTP."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "; same loop, different towers\n"
            "[under-test goal]    ; scripted model + mocked tools, deterministic\n"
            "[under-trace goal]   ; records every model/tool/approval call\n"
            "[explore goal]       ; multi-shot: every approve/deny world\n"
            "[run-resume journal] ; durable: replay the journal, then continue live"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "Foundation work"]
        [p
          "Getting here meant hardening the core: multi-shot continuations and correct handler-stack isolation on the register VM, generic ADT construction and escaping-handler typing in the checker, host effects (clock, uuid, env, files) and TCP sockets in the VM, working multi-file imports, and several higher-order builtin fixes. A new backend differential-parity suite runs the same programs across backends and turns any divergence into a failing test."]]
      
      ; ── v0.6.0 — Evidence IR & Register VM ─────────────────────────────
      [blog-article
        "The Register VM"
        "Mar 23, 2026"
        
        [p
          "Loon v0.6 replaces the tree-walking interpreter with a register-based virtual machine. Programs compile to Evidence IR — a flat, typed intermediate representation — then execute on a NaN-boxed register VM. The result: 4x faster interpretation, with a Cranelift native backend that hits 370x on compute-heavy code."]
        
        [blog-h3 "Evidence IR"]
        [p
          "EIR is a register-based IR where every value lives in a virtual register. Functions lower to blocks of flat instructions — no recursion, no tree walks. The compiler resolves closures, pattern matching, and effect dispatch at compile time."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "; source\n"
            "[fn add [a b] [+ a b]]\n"
            "\n"
            "; lowers to\n"
            ";   r0 = param 0    (a)\n"
            ";   r1 = param 1    (b)\n"
            ";   r2 = add r0, r1\n"
            ";   ret r2"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "NaN-boxing"]
        [p
          "Every value fits in 8 bytes. Floats are stored as-is. Integers, booleans, nil, and heap pointers are encoded in the NaN payload bits. No allocation for small values, no type tags to check — the IEEE 754 NaN space gives us 51 bits of payload for free."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "; all of these are a single u64:\n"
            "42          ; tagged integer\n"
            "3.14        ; raw f64\n"
            "true        ; tagged boolean\n"
            ":hello      ; interned symbol index"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "Three backends"]
        [p "EIR compiles to three targets from the same IR:"]
        [ul-el
          {:style {:margin-left "1.5rem" :line-height "1.8"}}
          [li-el
            "Register VM — 4x faster than the tree-walker, portable, debuggable"]
          [li-el
            "WASM — runs in browsers and edge workers via wasm32 codegen"]
          [li-el
            "Cranelift — native x86-64/aarch64, up to 370x on numeric code"]]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "loon run fib.oo             ; VM (default)\n"
            "loon run --native fib.oo    ; Cranelift\n"
            "loon build --wasm fib.oo    ; WASM module"]]
        
        [blog-h3 "Trace recorder"]
        [p
          "The VM includes a trace recorder that captures hot loop iterations — instruction sequences, types, branch decisions. This is the foundation for a future tracing JIT: record a trace, type-specialize it, emit native code via Cranelift, guard on type assumptions."]
        [blog-code
          [str
            "loon run --trace fib.oo\n"
            "; Trace 0: 12 instructions, 3 guards\n"
            ";   loop_header -> int_add -> int_sub -> branch ..."]]
        
        [blog-h3 "Benchmarks"]
        [p "Release mode, Apple M4 Max. Shorter bars are faster."]
        
        ; ── benchmark chart helper: each bar is 2 divs (row + fill) ──
        ; bar: [label, width%, time, color]
        ; ── fib(35) ──
        [div
          {:style {:font-family [font :mono]
              :font-size "0.8rem"
              :color [color :muted]
              :margin "1.5rem 0 0.4rem"}}
          "fib(35) — exponential recursion"]
        [div
